window.JG_DB["Maokai"] = {"Bel'Veth":{"tldr":"Bel'Veth adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Bel'Veth seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Bel'Veth Favored","why":"Bel'Veth's Void Surge (Q) and Above and Below (W) are both free-cost and cheap on cooldown, letting her dash in, knock up, and slow before Maokai has any hard lockdown of his own — Twisted Advance's root does not unlock until level 3. Bramble Smash only knocks back and slows, so it can push her out of range but cannot punish the dive itself, leaving Maokai on the losing side of an early skirmish or invade."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Bel'Veth. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Bel'Veth's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Bel'Veth for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Bel'Veth's first item turns the dash chain into sustained damage — she stops trading and starts running people down."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Bel'Veth wins any jungle fight against you before level 6, and Void Surge dashes every 4 seconds, so Bramble Smash rarely keeps her away for long. Your job is lanes, not duels. Clear three camps toward the lane with the best setup and gank at level 3 with Twisted Advance into Bramble Smash. Throw Sapling Toss into the brush at your raptors so you see her coming, since the sapling spots anything that walks past. Let her take the first crab, and recall after your gank.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Bel'Veth is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Bel'Veth takes scuttle quickly with Death in Lavender attack speed and can dash out of the contest the moment it turns.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Bel'Veth if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Bel'Veth wants every Void Grub and Rift Herald for the coral, because true form is what her whole game is built on.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Bel'Veth's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Bel'Veth holds four charges of escape, so an invade only works if you cut the dash line rather than chase it.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Count her Void Surge charges — with all four spent she is a melee champion with no way out of the pit.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Bel'Veth's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Bel'Veth is weakest before she banks a coral, when her health pool is small and her stacks have not built.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Bel'Veth takes side waves happily, because the attack-speed stacks make her a fast pusher as well as a duellist.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Bel'Veth picks come from dashing through a wall onto someone already slowed, so watch the terrain behind you.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Bel'Veth wins by banking a coral early, entering true form on an objective, and stacking through a fight nobody can end quickly."},"Diana":{"tldr":"Diana adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Diana seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Diana Favored","why":"Diana's Pale Cascade (W) grants a shield that absorbs Maokai's Q and auto-attack damage, and Crescent Strike (Q) at 900 range reveals him before he can retaliate. Maokai's Q only knocks back and slows, and without Twisted Advance's root until level 3 he has no way to punish a shielded all-in, so Diana can trade into an invade or skirmish and come out ahead."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Diana. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Diana's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Diana for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Diana's first item turns the Q-dash-Moonfall rotation from a chunk into a kill on anything without an escape."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Diana is even with you at levels 1 and 2, and at level 3 she wins with all three spells. She farms and rarely looks for you before level 6. Full clear, and only contest the crab if Crescent Strike has already missed, because a hit lets Lunar Rush dash to you for free. Twisted Advance roots her from 525 units, which is enough to stop her Pale Cascade combo. Gank a lane at level 3 instead of fighting her, and ward her second buff with a sapling.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Diana is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Diana contests scuttle with Crescent Strike poke rather than a fight, and commits only once Lunar Rush is up.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Diana if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Diana takes objectives quickly with the passive triple-strike, but her smite fight is weak until her ultimate is online.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Diana's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Diana can only dash to something she has marked, so invading before Crescent Strike lands takes away her escape.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The whole kit keys off Crescent Strike — if the arc misses, the dash and the pull are both off the table.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Diana's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Diana is weakest before six, when her clear is excellent and her kill threat genuinely is not.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Diana pushes a side lane very fast off her passive and is a real threat to anyone who answers it alone.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Diana picks are a Moonfall pull on someone standing near a wall, which is why she wants the fight in a corridor.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Diana wins by reaching six and two items and then one-rotating whichever squishy steps a pace too far forward."},"Ekko":{"tldr":"Ekko adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Ekko seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Ekko Favored","why":"Ekko's Timewinder (Q) slows and damages from 1075 range and Phase Dive (E) closes the distance for a burst reset, letting him poke and dive before Maokai can respond in kind. Bramble Smash only knocks back and slows at 600 range, and Maokai still has no hard lockdown until Twisted Advance unlocks at level 3, so Ekko can dictate an early skirmish or invade."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Ekko. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Ekko's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Ekko for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Ekko's first item is the point where his dive stops being a trade and becomes a kill, with the rewind as insurance."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Ekko is even with you through level 3. His first clear costs him health, while Sap Magic keeps yours up, so you arrive at the crab healthier. Timewinder slows you from 1075 units, but Bramble Smash knocks him back out of his own field. Full clear, take the crab on your side, and look for a level-3 gank with Twisted Advance, which he cannot dodge with Phase Dive once you are attached. Put a sapling in his raptor brush and recall after the crab.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Ekko is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Ekko wants the scuttle fight only if Parallel Convergence is already drawn on the crab — without the stun he backs off.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Ekko if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Ekko is a poor early smite fighter, so objectives before six are usually contestable if you arrive with health.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Ekko's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Ekko holds Phase Dive and Chronobreak, so a successful invade needs to land before six or with both of those tracked.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Watch for the Parallel Convergence ring on the ground — everything he does afterwards is timed to that stun.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Ekko's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Ekko is weakest between three and six, when his clear costs health and Chronobreak is not there to undo a mistake.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Ekko is one of the better side laners in the jungle, because he can dive the wave and rewind out of the answer.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Ekko picks come from a W stun placed behind you in a choke, so cross open ground rather than corridors when he is missing.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Ekko wins by taking dives nobody else can take and rewinding out of the punish until the lead compounds."},"Elise":{"tldr":"Elise adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Elise seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Elise Favored","why":"Elise can Cocoon (E) stun from 1075 range to open a fight and follow with Human Form Neurotoxin (Q) that scales off Maokai's high current Health, punishing his tanky HP pool. Bramble Smash only knocks back and slows, and Maokai has no hard CC of his own until Twisted Advance unlocks at level 3, so Elise dictates the terms of an early skirmish or invade."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Elise. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Elise's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Elise for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Elise's first item keeps the spider-form burst relevant a while longer, but it is the last window where it one-shots."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Elise wins every early fight and wants to gank at level 3 with Cocoon, which stuns from 1075 units. You counter-gank her better than you duel her. Full clear toward the lane she is most likely to visit, and throw Sapling Toss into the river brush she would walk through. When she lands Cocoon on a laner, Twisted Advance onto her before Rappel is up. Let her have the first crab, and recall after the counter-gank.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Elise is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Elise contests scuttle hard — a landed Cocoon at that timer is usually a kill and not just a crab.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Elise if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Elise has poor objective damage for her power level, so a drake she takes alone is slow and interruptible.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Elise's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Elise escapes an invade with Rappel, which makes her untargetable, so the punish has to land before that E is up.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Cocoon is the whole matchup — if it misses, spider form has to walk into you with no way back out.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Elise's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Elise is weakest after the mid game, when the Cocoon still lands but the burst behind it stops killing.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Elise does not side lane; she wants to be near her team, where a pick turns straight into an objective.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Elise picks are a Cocoon from a bush at a river entrance, so sweep before you cross and never walk the same line twice.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Elise wins by cashing her level-three and mid-game power into objectives before her damage falls behind."},"Evelynn":{"tldr":"Evelynn adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Evelynn seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Evelynn Favored","why":"Evelynn is invisible while out of combat via Demon Shade and can pick her moment to land Hate Spike (Q) poke or set up Allure (W)'s delayed charm before Maokai even sees her coming. Bramble Smash only knocks back and slows enemies already in range, and Maokai has no way to reveal her and no hard CC of his own until Twisted Advance unlocks at level 3, so an ambush invade at level 2 favors Evelynn."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Evelynn. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Evelynn's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Evelynn for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Evelynn's first item plus camouflage is the spike that flips the matchup — the charm rotation starts killing outright."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Evelynn has no stealth and no escape before level 6, and she clears slowly, so you are favoured even as a tank. Full clear, then take the crab near her side. Twisted Advance roots her from 525 units, and Bramble Smash knocks her back into your laner. Allure charms you after a wind-up on a 15-second cooldown. Put a sapling in her raptor brush to track her, invade her second buff with your mid, and recall before her level 6.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Evelynn is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Evelynn will not contest a scuttle before six unless she has numbers — an unstealthed Evelynn loses that fight.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Evelynn if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Evelynn cannot solo objectives early, so anything before her ultimate is a timer you can take with confidence.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Evelynn's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Evelynn is the best invade target in the jungle before six; after six she simply is not there to be invaded.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. From six on, treat every unswept bush as occupied — the charm is the start of everything she does.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Evelynn's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Evelynn is weakest from one to five, and it is not close; this matchup is decided by how hard you punish that.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Evelynn does not hold a side lane, she hunts one — a laner alone on that side is the bait, not the answer.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Evelynn picks are a charm out of camouflage onto whoever walked without a sweeper, and that is the whole late game.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Evelynn wins by surviving to six, then removing one person per fight before the fight has properly started."},"Graves":{"tldr":"Graves adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Graves seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Graves Favored","why":"Graves already has End of the Line (Q) and a ranged auto-attack that outrange and outburst Maokai's short-range Bramble Smash (Q) in a straight fight. If Graves commits to your jungle at this level, his shell damage plus autos punishes you before Q's knockback can create separation, so disengage into a brush rather than trade. This window favors Graves — Maokai's tools here are for peeling and clear, not for winning a duel against ranged burst."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Graves. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Graves's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Graves for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a persistent area burn plus health that speeds up your clear and adds free damage in extended fights. Your camp clears become near-automatic, letting you contest neutral objectives more aggressively, though this doesn't change the core matchup problem: Graves still poke you down at range before you can close with Twisted Advance (W). This priority mainly helps you hold objective pits rather than win a straight duel. Graves' first item is a large step in raw damage — the double-shot at point blank starts removing squishies outright."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Graves has one of the fastest clears in the game and wins every early fight, and he invades slow junglers. Start away from his side and put a sapling in your raptor brush before he arrives. End of the Line and his shotgun beat your Sap Magic healing in a camp. Quickdraw gives him a dash every 16 seconds. Let him have the first crab. Gank at level 3 with Twisted Advance into Bramble Smash instead, and fight Graves only with a laner.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Graves is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Graves takes scuttle fast and wins the contest at point-blank range, where both shells of a reload land on you.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Graves if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Graves is an excellent objective duellist because Smoke Screen blinds your smite fight and his burst is front-loaded.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Graves's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Invade Graves in the open rather than in his buff pit, because True Grit armour makes him the stronger side of a close-quarters brawl.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Count his shells: a Graves mid-reload has no burst, and that is the only safe second to step into him.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Graves's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Graves is weakest in open ground at range, where his pellets spread and he cannot make the auto-attacks count.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Graves is a strong side laner who holds a wave against most of the map and pushes it fast when left alone.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Graves picks come from a Smoke Screen dropped on a choke, so never walk a corridor he already has vision of.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Graves wins by taking your camps, holding a side lane, and shredding anyone who answers him in a tight space."},"Kayn":{"tldr":"Kayn adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Kayn seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Bramble Smash (Q) comes online with a 7-second cooldown, giving you a real knockup and slow to punish a contested camp or an early Kayn dive. At level 2 Kayn only has Reaping Slash (Q), a single dash-and-slash with no hard CC, so he cannot force a fight here without eating your knockback. If he tries an uncalculated invade, drag the camp to a brush and let Q peel him off while Sapling Toss whittles his health."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Kayn. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Kayn's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Kayn for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Kayn's first item lands around the same time as his form, which is why that window is a sharp jump and not a slope."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Kayn out-duels you early, and Shadow Step lets him reach your camps through walls on a 21-second cooldown, past the brush your saplings watch. He mainly wants orbs, so he may avoid you. Full clear on your side and gank at level 3 with Twisted Advance, which still roots him if he is in terrain nearby. Let him have the crab if he arrives first. Bramble Smash knocks him out of Reaping Slash range. Recall after your gank.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Kayn is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Kayn contests scuttle only when the orb count needs it — before a form he would rather take the safe crab.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Kayn if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Kayn is a weak early objective fighter, so pre-form pits are a timer you can take against him.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Kayn's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Kayn walks out of any invade through a wall with Shadow Step, so the only punish is catching him mid-camp.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The form is the clock: check which orbs he is taking, because that decides whether he brawls or bursts.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Kayn's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Kayn is weakest pre-form, when his stats lag every jungler on the roster and his kit is only mobility.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Kayn side lanes very well once formed, and the wall-walk means answering him alone rarely ends with a kill.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Kayn picks come out of a wall you did not ward, so treat every piece of terrain near him as an entrance.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Kayn wins by surviving to his form and then either bursting your carry or brawling through your front line."},"Kha'Zix":{"tldr":"Kha'Zix adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Kha'Zix seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Bramble Smash (Q) comes online with a 7-second cooldown, giving you a real knockup and slow to punish a contested camp. At level 2 Kha'Zix only has Taste Their Fear (Q), a short-range poke with no CC and no gap closer yet — Leap (E) is still locked — so he cannot force a clean fight here. If he tries an uncalculated invade, drag the camp to a brush and let Q knock him off while Sapling Toss chips his health."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Kha'Zix. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Kha'Zix's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Kha'Zix for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Kha'Zix's first item plus the first evolution is the spike — an isolated squishy stops surviving the opener."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Kha'Zix wins the early fight and he looks for isolated targets, which a Maokai clearing alone often is. Taste Their Fear deals more damage when nobody is next to you, and Leap reaches 700 units on a 20-second cooldown. Clear the camps closest to your lanes first so a laner is near. Throw Sapling Toss into the brush around your raptors to see him walk in. Let him take the first crab, gank at level 3 with Twisted Advance, and fight him only with a laner beside you.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Kha'Zix is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Kha'Zix contests scuttle happily if you are alone at it, because the isolation bonus turns the crab into a kill.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Kha'Zix if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Kha'Zix has strong single-target objective damage but a poor pit fight against anyone with crowd control.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Kha'Zix's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Kha'Zix escapes an invade with Leap and, once evolved, with the ultimate's stealth, so cut the escape angle first.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Isolation is the whole thing — near a camp or an ally his Q does far less, so never face-check alone.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Kha'Zix's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Kha'Zix is weakest in a grouped fight, where nothing is isolated and his burst has no preferred target.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Kha'Zix side lanes to look for a lone answer rather than to push, so the wave is bait as often as not.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Kha'Zix picks are an evolved leap onto whoever walked a corridor alone, which is why vision on side entrances matters.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Kha'Zix wins by finding an isolated target every fight and deleting it before the fight is joined."},"Kindred":{"tldr":"Kindred adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Kindred seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Bramble Smash (Q) comes online with a 7-second cooldown, giving you a real knockup and slow to punish a contested camp. At level 2 Kindred only has Dance of Arrows (Q), a short ranged tumble-and-shoot with no CC, so she has no way to force or escape a fight if you close with Q. If she tries an uncalculated invade, drag the camp to a brush and let Q knock her back while saplings chip her health."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Kindred. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Kindred's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Kindred for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Kindred's first item plus a few stacks is where the ranged trade stops being poke and starts being a duel they win."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Kindred wins the early fight, and their hunts bring them into your jungle to take camps. Their range means you never reach them without Twisted Advance, which only has 525 units of range. Put a sapling next to the camp closest to their side so you see them arrive. Dance of Arrows tumbles them out of your root on a 9-second cooldown. The first crab is theirs; you cannot reach a Kindred who shoots the crab from range. Gank at level 3 instead, and fight Kindred only once the tumble is used and a laner is with you.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Kindred is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Kindred wins scuttle contests by kiting rather than fighting — three dashes banked means the crab is theirs.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Kindred if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Kindred takes objectives quickly with the passive attack speed and can hold a pit with Lamb's Respite.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Kindred's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Kindred is hard to catch in an invade, since every Q is a dash and the jungle terrain works in their favour.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Watch the mark: a live mark on your side of the map says exactly where Kindred is going next.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Kindred's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Kindred is weakest with no marks stacked and no dashes banked, which is when their damage is simply low.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Kindred side lanes safely because the kiting pattern beats anything that has to walk at them.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Kindred does not pick so much as collect, so the danger is chasing them into a Lamb's Respite you cannot leave.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Kindred wins by stacking marks early and turning every late fight into a kite their team fights inside."},"Lee Sin":{"tldr":"Lee Sin adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Lee Sin seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Lee Sin Favored","why":"Lee Sin unlocks Sonic Wave / Resonating Strike (Q) at level 2, a long-range poke-and-dash that lets him commit to a fight or bail out of your jungle on a short cooldown, with Flurry rewarding the follow-up attacks. Your own Bramble Smash (Q) only comes online this same level, so you cannot yet out-tempo his dash — hold Q for the moment he commits the Resonating Strike gap-close rather than using it early. This is genuinely his window, not yours."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Lee Sin. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Lee Sin's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Lee Sin for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Lee Sin's first item is his last real jump — after it his damage stays flat while everyone else keeps climbing."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Lee Sin wins the early fight and often invades. Sonic Wave reaches 1100 units, and Tempest slows you, so he catches you in your own camps. Start on the side away from his start and throw a sapling into your raptor brush. Lee is strongest at the crab timer, so let it go. Your level-3 gank with Twisted Advance and Bramble Smash is the better plan. If he invades, Bramble Smash knocks him back so you can walk toward your laner.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Lee Sin is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Lee Sin fights for every scuttle — the Q re-cast plus Flurry resets are at their strongest at exactly that timer.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Lee Sin if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Lee Sin is a strong early objective contester because Dragon's Rage can kick you out of the pit entirely.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Lee Sin's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Invade Lee Sin with his energy spent, because ward-hops let him reach — and leave — camps you did not expect him at.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Everything starts with Sonic Wave — stand behind a camp so it cannot connect and half his kit is idle.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Lee Sin's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Lee Sin is weakest from two items on, when his burst stops killing and his utility is all that is left.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Lee Sin side lanes poorly late; he wants the mid game, where a kick decides a fight before it starts.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Lee Sin picks are a Q from fog into a kick that separates you from your team, so hold your dash for the second hit.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Lee Sin wins by converting an early lead into objectives before the two-item mark takes his damage away."},"Naafiri":{"tldr":"Naafiri adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Naafiri seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Naafiri Favored","why":"Naafiri now has Darkin Daggers (Q) and The Call of the Pack (W), letting her and her packmates burst a target down before Maokai gets a W root online. Maokai only has Bramble Smash (Q) at this point — a knockback-slow, not a lockdown — so a level 2 skirmish favors Naafiri's pack damage over Maokai's single disengage tool. Play it safe and let saplings scout instead of contesting a straight fight."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Naafiri. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Naafiri's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Naafiri for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Naafiri's first item is where the dagger bleed plus the pack becomes a full health bar rather than a heavy trade."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Naafiri wins the early fight, and her packmates add damage to every trade. Bramble Smash knocks the whole pack back, but she still out-damages you. Hounds' Pursuit dashes at you on a 26-second cooldown. Start away from her side and put a sapling in your raptor brush. Let her take the first crab. Gank at level 3 with Twisted Advance, and counter-gank when she goes to a lane, where your root holds her for your laner.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Naafiri is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Naafiri wins a scuttle contest on numbers — the dogs make a level-two or level-three crab fight a 3v1.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Naafiri if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Naafiri has solid single-target objective damage but no way to hold a pit against a champion with a knock-up.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Naafiri's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Naafiri answers an invade with Hounds' Pursuit, which is untargetable, so kill the packmates before you commit.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Count the dogs — a Naafiri with no packmates up has lost most of her damage until they respawn.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Naafiri's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Naafiri is weakest with the pack dead, which is why clearing them first changes the fight more than any cooldown.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Naafiri side lanes fast and is a genuine 1v1 threat, but she gives up the fight around objectives to do it.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Naafiri picks are a W dash onto anyone she can already reach with a slow, so break the slow before the dash lands.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Naafiri wins by picking one target per fight and having the pack finish it before your team can rotate."},"Nidalee":{"tldr":"Nidalee adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Nidalee seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Nidalee Favored","why":"Nidalee has Javelin Toss (Q) at 1500 range and can already threaten a cougar-form Takedown execute on low-health targets, while Maokai's only tool is Bramble Smash (Q) — a short-range knockback with no way to close the gap before W. A confirmed Nidalee spear poke or a Pounce-in cougar dive punishes Maokai's clear before he can react, so this level is hers."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Nidalee. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Nidalee's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Nidalee for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Nidalee's first item makes the spear a genuine execute threat rather than chip damage on the way to a fight."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Nidalee wins the early fight and steals camps from slow junglers. Javelin Toss reaches 1500 units, far beyond anything you have. Keep a monster between you and her spear. Pounce has a 13-second cooldown. Start away from her side and put a sapling in your raptor brush, where she usually invades. She reaches the crab first and spears you on the way in, so leave it. Gank at level 3 instead. Twisted Advance can catch her after she Pounces in.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Nidalee is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Nidalee decides scuttle contests with a max-range Javelin before either of you is standing on the crab.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Nidalee if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Nidalee has excellent objective damage from the hunt bonus but cannot hold a pit against a real duellist.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Nidalee's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Nidalee cannot be caught in an invade — the pounce reset means she leaves through terrain you cannot follow.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The Javelin is the tell: a landed spear means the pounce is coming, so break line of sight instead of walking back.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Nidalee's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Nidalee is weakest at close range with her cooldowns spent, where a cougar with no pounce has to fight honestly.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Nidalee does not hold a side lane; she poke-clears it and leaves before anyone can arrive.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Nidalee picks are a spear out of fog followed by a pounce, which is why the spear line matters more than her position.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Nidalee wins by poking a target below the hunt threshold and executing it before the fight begins properly."},"Nocturne":{"tldr":"Nocturne adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Nocturne seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Nocturne Favored","why":"Nocturne's Duskbringer (Q) hits from 1125 range and grants him bonus move speed and attack damage on the trail, letting him close and burst before Maokai's short-range Q knockback can peel him off. Umbra Blades also gives Nocturne a heal-on-hit reset that outsustains Maokai's single-target Sap Magic in a straight trade. Without Twisted Advance (W) yet, Maokai has no way to lock Nocturne down, so this skirmish window belongs to Nocturne."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Nocturne. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Nocturne's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Nocturne for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Nocturne's first item is where the Duskbringer trade stops being a chunk and becomes a kill on any squishy."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Nocturne wins the early fight and heals through camps with Umbra Blades, so he is never low when he finds you. Unspeakable Horror fears you if you stay within 425 units, and Shroud of Darkness blocks one spell, which is often your Twisted Advance. Start away from his side and put a sapling in your blue brush. The crab is his early. Spend your level-3 route on a gank, and when you do meet him, bait the shield with Bramble Smash before you root him.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Nocturne is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Nocturne contests scuttle on the Duskbringer trail, where the movement speed and the fear make the trade one-sided.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Nocturne if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Nocturne is a good single-target objective taker with the passive heal but has no zone control in the pit.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Nocturne's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Nocturne's spell shield makes an invade awkward — bait it with a low-value ability before committing the real one.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Watch the spell shield: while it is up your engage is refunded, and while it is down he is an ordinary melee.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Nocturne's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Nocturne is weakest against a team that stays grouped, since the fear tether breaks on distance and needs isolation.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Nocturne side lanes well and threatens to leave it at any moment, because his ultimate reaches the whole map.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Nocturne picks are a Paranoia dive onto someone he has already chunked, so hug terrain near allies on the cast.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Nocturne wins by darkening the map and arriving on the one person your team cannot afford to lose."},"Rengar":{"tldr":"Rengar adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Rengar seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Bramble Smash (Q) gives you a knockback and slow that Rengar has no way to punish through before he has Ferocity stacked from Unseen Predator jumps, since none of his basics apply hard CC on their own. If he tries an early invade, drag the camp toward a brush and use Q to knock him off and disengage while your saplings chip his health. Rengar has no reliable all-in before his ultimate, so this window still favors you."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Rengar. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Rengar's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Rengar for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Rengar's first item is the point where an empowered Savagery stops chunking and starts one-shotting squishier targets."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Rengar wins the early fight and starts every fight from brush. Sapling Toss is your best answer: a sapling in the bush next to your camp spots him and attacks him if he waits there. Bola Strike slows you from 1000 units and roots at full Ferocity. Start away from his side and throw saplings into the brush near your first camps. The crab sits beside river brush, so leave it to him. Take your level-3 gank with Twisted Advance into a lane he is far from.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Rengar is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Rengar contests scuttle from a brush — a leap opener at that timer is usually a kill rather than a trade.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Rengar if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Rengar has good single-target objective damage but nothing that holds a pit if the fight goes long.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Rengar's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Rengar invades through brush lines, so the bushes on your side of the jungle are the ones that matter.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The ferocity count is the tell — at four stacks the next ability is the empowered one, and that is the burst.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Rengar's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Rengar is weakest in the middle of an open corridor, where there is no bush to open from and no reset to chain.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Rengar side lanes to hunt whoever answers rather than to push, so treat the wave as bait until proven otherwise.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Rengar picks are a stealth leap out of Thrill of the Hunt, so control wards do more for you than health does.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Rengar wins by removing a squishy at the start of every fight from a bush your team did not sweep."},"Shaco":{"tldr":"Shaco adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Shaco seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Bramble Smash (Q) knocks back and slows, and its cast covers you the instant Shaco's Deceive brings him in from stealth for a backstab auto — you don't need to see him to punish the entry. If he Deceives into your camp early, drag it to a brush and Q on his arrival before Backstab damage lands. Shaco has no hard CC of his own yet at level 2, so a clean Q reaction still wins the trade."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Shaco. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Shaco's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Shaco for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Shaco's first item is where the Deceive opener plus box damage becomes a one-rotation kill on squishier targets."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Shaco wins the early fight, and Jack In The Box can be hidden in your camp before you arrive. Deceive has a 13-second cooldown and makes him invisible. Throw a sapling into your first camp's brush to check for boxes. Start away from his likely start and clear quickly. Give him the first crab. Gank at level 3 with Twisted Advance. Bramble Smash knocks him away if he backstabs you.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Shaco is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Shaco contests scuttle with boxes already down, which makes the crab a 3v1 rather than a duel.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Shaco if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Shaco has strong single-target objective damage off Backstab but folds in a pit fight he cannot open from stealth.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Shaco's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Shaco invades level one more than almost anyone, so the fear is the boxes at your buff rather than the champion.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Boxes are the map: a cleared box line tells you exactly which entrance he intends to fight at.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Shaco's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Shaco is weakest in an even fight with no boxes and no clone, which is the only version worth taking.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Shaco side lanes to threaten a backdoor rather than to duel, and the clone makes answering him a guess.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Shaco picks are a Deceive from behind onto someone alone, so keep a wall at your back near his side of the map.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Shaco wins by making every second of the game confusing and cashing one clean pick per fight."},"Talon":{"tldr":"Talon adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Talon seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Talon Favored","why":"Noxian Diplomacy (Q) lets Talon leap in for crit damage and Rake (W) adds a knockup and slow on top, giving him a full burst combo before you have any way to peel — your only CC tool this early is the knockback on Bramble Smash (Q), and it fires after his damage already lands. If he commits to an invade, your best play is to disengage into a brush rather than trade, since he can refund health and cooldown on Q if the kill lands. This window still belongs to his burst."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Talon. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Talon's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Talon for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Talon's first item is the point where the bleed rotation goes from a heavy chunk to a full kill on a squishy."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Talon wins the early fight, and Assassin's Path lets him vault walls into your camps from angles a sapling in the river will not see. Noxian Diplomacy leaps from 575 units. Put your sapling inside your jungle, on the path between your buff and raptors. Start away from his side. He usually roams a lane at level 3, so counter-gank there: Twisted Advance roots him from 525 units while your laner hits. Leave the first crab to him and recall after the counter-gank.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Talon is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Talon contests scuttle by arriving over a wall you did not expect, and leaves the same way if it turns.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Talon if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Talon has decent single-target objective damage but no way to hold a pit against anyone with crowd control.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Talon's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Talon cannot really be trapped — Assassin's Path takes him over terrain no chase can follow.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Track his wall-hop cooldowns per wall: a wall he has just crossed is one he cannot cross again immediately.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Talon's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Talon is weakest in a grouped fight, where the burst has no isolated target and the stealth has nowhere to end.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Talon side lanes to find a solo kill and rotate over walls, so he is a threat to two lanes at once.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Talon picks are a wall-hop into a full rotation on whoever is furthest forward, so never be that person.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Talon wins by removing an isolated carry and leaving over terrain before anyone can trade back."},"Viego":{"tldr":"Viego adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Viego seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Viego Favored","why":"Viego's second ability, Spectral Maw (W), adds a dash into a stun on top of Blade of the Ruined King's current-health damage, letting him threaten a level-2 all-in your single Bramble Smash (Q) can't answer alone. Your Q knockback still punishes a careless dive by disengaging into a brush, but in an even fight Viego's stun-into-execute-percent-health combo beats your peel tool at this level."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Viego. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Viego's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Viego for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Viego's first item is where the sustained damage becomes a duel he wins outright rather than a trade he survives."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Viego wins the early fight and heals on every hit with Blade of the Ruined King, so your Sap Magic sustain loses a long trade. Spectral Maw charges before it fires and stuns the first enemy hit, on an 8-second cooldown. Open on the buff farthest from his start and drop a sapling in your raptor brush. Do not walk to the crab while he is on it. Gank at level 3 with Twisted Advance, and if he invades, knock him back with Bramble Smash and walk toward a lane.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Viego is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Viego contests scuttle with the Spectral Maw stun, and the crab fight is decided by whether that charge lands.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Viego if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Viego is a good objective duellist with sustained damage but has no way to zone you off the pit.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Viego's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Viego escapes an invade through Harrowed Path, so the mist trails on the map are the routes he actually uses.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Dodge the Maw charge-up — without the stun his early all-in is a set of ordinary auto-attacks.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Viego's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Viego is weakest in a fight where nothing dies, since every reset he gets depends on a body being available.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Viego side lanes well and the possession makes answering him with one person risky if a wave is dying nearby.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Viego picks come out of the mist onto someone already low, so treat any mist trail as an occupied corridor.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Viego wins by turning one won fight into three through resets, which is why the first kill matters most."},"Wukong":{"tldr":"Wukong adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Wukong seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Wukong Favored","why":"Wukong's Crushing Blow (Q) empowered auto and Stone Skin passive (stacking armor and health regen while fighting) let him win an extended trade the instant he has a second ability online, before your Bramble Smash (Q) alone offsets his armor shred and sustain. Your Q knockback can still punish a careless invade by disengaging into a brush, but a straight fight favors Wukong's dueling tools at this level."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Wukong. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Wukong's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Wukong for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Wukong's first item is where the armour shred plus Crushing Blow becomes a duel he wins rather than survives."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Wukong is the stronger duelist before level 6. Crushing Blow shreds your armour, and Nimbus Strike dashes to you from 650 units. Warrior Trickster leaves a clone, and your saplings attack the clone as happily as the real Wukong. Start away from his side and put a sapling in your raptor brush. He is stronger at the crab, so skip it. Gank at level 3 with Twisted Advance, and use Bramble Smash to knock him away if he dives your laner.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Wukong is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Wukong contests scuttle with a clone bluff as often as with damage — the crab is won on the guess, not the duel.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Wukong if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Wukong takes objectives at a fair pace and can knock you out of the pit twice with a double-cast ultimate.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Wukong's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Wukong escapes an invade by leaving a clone behind, so commit your key ability only after the real one is confirmed.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The clone walks in a straight line and does not react; the real Wukong is the one that changes direction.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Wukong's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Wukong is weakest with the clone spent, when he is a melee bruiser with one dash and no bluff left.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Wukong side lanes well and the clone makes a 1v1 answer into a coin flip you would rather not take.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Wukong picks are a Nimbus Strike into a double knock-up on a group, so spread before the spin rather than during.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Wukong wins by catching two or more people in the ultimate at the moment his team is ready to follow."},"Briar":{"tldr":"Briar adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Briar seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Briar Favored","why":"Briar already has two hard-CC dashes at level 2, Head Rush (Q) and Blood Frenzy (W), both stunning on arrival and both free of mana cost, plus a lifesteal-heavy passive that heals off every hit she lands. Maokai's Q only knocks back and slows, and Twisted Advance's root is still a level away, so Briar can leap in, stun, and sustain through the trade while Maokai has nothing that locks her down first."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Briar. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Briar's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Briar for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Briar's first item is a step change: the frenzy lifesteal starts out-healing full combos rather than just chip damage."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Briar wins the early fight, and Blood Frenzy heals her while she chases the nearest target. Head Rush stuns from 475 units on a 13-second cooldown. Bramble Smash knocks her back, which buys time but does not stop the frenzy. Start away from her side, put a sapling at your raptors, and let her keep the crab. Gank at level 3 instead. Your root is at its best in a counter-gank, when her frenzy has carried her under a tower.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Briar is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Contest scuttle against Briar with the health lead or not at all, because the frenzy lifesteal out-sustains most trades at that timer.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Briar if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Briar takes objectives fast but cannot walk away from one: once the W is spent she is locked into the fight.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Briar's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Invade Briar only with her frenzy on cooldown — Head Rush and the healing turn a fair 1v1 in her jungle into a kill for her.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Watch her frenzy state; while she is frenzied she cannot be told where to go, and that is the moment to leave.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Briar's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Briar is weakest when her frenzy is on cooldown and she has no health to heal back from a fight she cannot leave.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Briar side lanes well because the sustain holds a wave through poke, but she gives up the rest of the map to do it.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Briar picks are Certain Death from across the screen, so stay near a wall and a teammate while her ultimate is up.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Briar wins by starting a fight her team follows into and healing through the answer until the health bars flip."},"Jax":{"tldr":"Jax adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Jax seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Jax Favored","why":"Jax already has Leap Strike (Q) to close the gap and Empower (W) to load a heavy burst auto, and he does not need Counter Strike (E) to win this trade. If he commits to your jungle, use Bramble Smash's knockback and slow to shove him off and disengage into a brush rather than standing to fight — this window favors Jax on burst, not Maokai."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Jax. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Jax's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Jax for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a persistent area burn plus health that speeds up your clear and adds free damage against a melee fighter like Jax, who has to stand next to you to stack Relentless Assault (P) or land Empower. Your camp clears become near-automatic, letting you contest neutral objectives more aggressively and hold your ground longer in extended trades. Jax's first item is a real threshold — the Empower-leap burst goes from a trade to a kill on squishier junglers."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Jax out-trades you in any early duel. Counter Strike dodges your attacks, then stuns everyone within 300 units, and Leap Strike reaches you from 700 units. Your root still lands during his stance, since Twisted Advance is not an attack. Begin away from his side, and put a sapling where he would Leap Strike in, beside your raptors. Do not fight him for the crab. Gank at level 3, and counter-gank his lane ganks by rooting him after his Leap Strike.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Jax is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Jax will take almost any scuttle contest, because the E stun turns a contested crab into a won duel.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Jax if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Jax is a slow objective taker early but a strong smite fighter, since the stun interrupts your attempt entirely.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Jax's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Invade Jax between camps rather than at one, since a camp beside him feeds Counter Strike just as your own attacks do.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Counter Strike has a two-second dodge window; the answer is to stop attacking and step out, not to trade through it.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Jax's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Jax is weakest with his E down and no camp beside him, which is the only version of this fight worth taking.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Jax is the best side laner in the jungle at full build and will take a 1v2 on that wave if you let him.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Jax does not pick people, he waits for one to walk into range, so respect his leap distance around walls.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Jax wins by getting to full build and holding a side lane that costs you two people to answer."},"Master Yi":{"tldr":"Master Yi adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Master Yi seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Master Yi Favored","why":"Master Yi's Alpha Strike (Q) is online at level 2 and makes him untargetable while dashing through multiple targets, dodging your Bramble Smash entirely if he times the cast. Double Strike stacking off basic attacks means a clean Alpha Strike engage into autos can win the trade before your Q ever lands. Play this window cautiously and save Q to punish him the instant Alpha Strike's animation ends rather than trying to zone him out of it."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Master Yi. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Master Yi's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Master Yi for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Master Yi's first item is the start of the curve rather than a spike — his real jump is the second and third."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Master Yi is even with you through level 3. He needs a clean full clear, while you want lanes. Twisted Advance roots him from 525 units and stops Meditate, and Bramble Smash slows him. Alpha Strike makes him untargetable on a 20-second cooldown, so root him after he lands. Full clear and take the crab on your side. Put a sapling in his raptor brush, gank at level 3, and invade his second buff with your mid.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Master Yi is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Master Yi avoids scuttle contests before six — he takes the free crab and gives up the contested one.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Master Yi if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Master Yi has strong single-target objective damage but a weak pit fight, so contest his smites with crowd control.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Master Yi's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Master Yi is one of the best invade targets in the game before six, since Meditate only delays the outcome.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Alpha Strike makes him untargetable for its duration; hold your key ability for after it ends, not during.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Master Yi's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Master Yi is weakest from one to five, and everything he does later depends on how badly that window went.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Master Yi side lanes at full build and answering him alone there is a mistake, however far ahead you are.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Master Yi does not pick; he cleans up, so the danger is a fight your team half-loses rather than an ambush.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Master Yi wins by farming to full build and then resetting Highlander through a fight nobody can chain-stun."},"Olaf":{"tldr":"Olaf adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Olaf seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Olaf Favored","why":"Olaf's Reckless Swing (E) deals true damage that ignores Maokai's armor entirely, and Berserker Rage gives him more attack speed and lifesteal the lower his health drops, so a prolonged trade only gets worse for Maokai. Bramble Smash (Q) can knock Olaf away, but it doesn't stop him from re-engaging once his axe (Undertow) has slowed the target, and Olaf can simply choose to fight through the knockback with his sustain. This skirmish window favors Olaf."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Olaf. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Olaf's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Olaf for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Olaf's first item is a large jump — the true damage plus lifesteal turns extended fights into ones he simply does not lose."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Olaf wins every early fight and he likes to invade. Undertow slows you from 1000 units and Reckless Swing deals true damage, which ignores the armour you rely on. Start on the side away from his start, and put a sapling at your second buff entrance before 2:30. If he walks in, Bramble Smash knocks him back and slows him while you leave toward a lane. Leave both crabs to him. Your level-3 gank still works, so spend your early game in lanes, not near him.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Olaf is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Contest scuttle against Olaf only from full health, since his sustain and true damage beat most of the roster at that timer.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Olaf if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Olaf is a strong objective duellist because Reckless Swing ignores resistances and his health stays high through the fight.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Olaf's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Invade Olaf above half health and never inside his slow, because a low-health 1v1 in his own jungle is the fight he wants.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The axe on the ground is the tell — an Olaf who cannot pick it back up has a long cooldown and no slow.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Olaf's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Olaf is weakest against distance: he has no gap-closer, so a wall between you and the axe line takes the fight away.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Olaf side lanes strongly and takes any 1v1 there, but he cannot answer a fight on the other side of the map.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Olaf does not pick; he runs people down, so the danger is a slow landing rather than an ambush.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Olaf wins by invading, snowballing early, and diving through crowd control before anyone can scale past him."},"Qiyana":{"tldr":"Qiyana adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Qiyana seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Qiyana Favored","why":"Qiyana already has Elemental Wrath (Q), a hard-CC element attack, and Audacity (E), a dash gap-closer, letting her burst in and out before Maokai's short-range Bramble Smash (Q) can react. Her Royal Privilege passive adds bonus damage on the first hit against Maokai in the fight, and Terrashape (W) grants an enchant that boosts her follow-up. Maokai still lacks Twisted Advance (W), so he has no way to punish the dash-in — this skirmish window belongs to Qiyana."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Qiyana. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Qiyana's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Qiyana for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Qiyana's first item is the threshold where the element combo goes from a heavy trade to a full deletion."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Qiyana wins the early fight, but she ganks more than she invades. Audacity dashes onto a target from 650 units, and Terrashape gives her a second dash on a 7-second cooldown. If she takes the grass element, she hides in brush, and a sapling there reveals her. Throw saplings into the river brush near your lanes. Avoid the crab, because the river gives her the water element that roots. Counter-gank her level-3 gank with Twisted Advance.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Qiyana is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Qiyana contests scuttle with the river element, where the slow makes the trade one-sided before it starts.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Qiyana if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Qiyana has poor objective damage and no way to hold a pit, so her smite fights are the weakest part of her game.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Qiyana's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Qiyana escapes an invade with Terrashape and Audacity, so cut the wall she is heading for rather than chasing.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Track which element she is holding — brush means stealth, river means slow, and rock means the wall stun is armed.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Qiyana's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Qiyana is weakest in open ground away from terrain, where the ultimate has nothing to stun against.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Qiyana side lanes to look for a solo kill rather than to push, and the wall-hop makes her hard to punish for it.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Qiyana picks are a brush-stealth Q onto someone alone, so sweep the bushes on the side she was last seen.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Qiyana wins by one-shotting a carry and using the wall stun to make the follow-up fight unwinnable."},"Shyvana":{"tldr":"Shyvana adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Shyvana seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Shyvana Favored","why":"Shyvana's Emberstrike (Q) deals true damage that ignores your resistances entirely, and Inferno Aegis (W) shields her through your Q knockback before it detonates for extra damage and Grievous-Wounds-relevant healing. Your Bramble Smash slow and knockup do not stop her from re-engaging once the shield drops, so a straight level-2 fight favors her sustained true damage over your burst. Play this window for scouting and camp control rather than a forced duel."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Shyvana. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Shyvana's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Shyvana for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Shyvana's first item plus dragon form is the sharpest spike she gets — the farm bot becomes a duellist in one moment."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Shyvana farms and does very little before level 6, so you are favoured. She clears fast and healthy and usually walks straight to the crab after her full clear. Take the crab first, and if she contests, Twisted Advance roots her and Bramble Smash knocks her toward your laner. Inferno Aegis shields her on a 13-second cooldown. Put a sapling in her second buff brush, invade it when she shows in a lane, and gank at level 3.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Shyvana is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Shyvana takes scuttle quickly but has no crowd control to win a contested one before her ultimate.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Shyvana if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Shyvana is an excellent drake taker, so treat every early drake she has vision on as already contested.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Shyvana's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Shyvana is one of the best invade targets before six — no crowd control, no escape, and a clear she cannot abandon.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Watch her fury bar: a full bar means the transform is available and the fight she takes will be a different one.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Shyvana's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Shyvana is weakest before six, and her whole game is built on how much farm she banked during that window.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Shyvana side lanes extremely well at full build and is a genuine 1v1 threat to anyone who answers her alone.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Shyvana picks are a dragon-form flight into a knock-back, so keep out of the flight line when her ultimate is up.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Shyvana wins by farming untouched to two items and turning into a stat check most junglers cannot answer."},"Trundle":{"tldr":"Trundle adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Trundle seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Trundle Favored","why":"Chomp (Q) resets Trundle's auto-attacks and saps your Attack Damage on every hit, and Frozen Domain (W) gives him bonus Attack Speed, Move Speed, and healing the moment he stands on his zone — a sustained trade he wins outright against your burst-light early kit. Bramble Smash (Q) can knock him off the fight, but he can reapply Frozen Domain and keep chasing. Treat this window as a disengage-only matchup rather than a fight you can win."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Trundle. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Trundle's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Trundle for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Trundle's first item is a real threshold — the sustain plus stat theft makes long fights ones he cannot lose."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Trundle is one of your worst early matchups. Chomp steals your damage every 3.5 seconds, and Subjugate later steals your armour and health as well, which is a bad trade for a tank. Keep saplings in the brush beside your buffs and clear quickly. If he invades, Bramble Smash knocks him out of Frozen Domain, then walk away before Pillar of Ice blocks your path. Skip the first crab, since he wants that fight. Take your level-3 gank in a lane far from his side, and recall right after it.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Trundle is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Contest scuttle against Trundle outside his Frozen Domain, because inside it the attack speed and stat steal decide the duel.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Trundle if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Trundle is an excellent objective duellist because Subjugate drains health and resistances at exactly the moment it matters.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Trundle's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Trundle invades and wins, so an invade against him only works with numbers or with him already low.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The pillar is the tell — once it is placed your retreat line is gone, so move before it goes down and not after.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Trundle's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Trundle is weakest outside his Frozen Domain, where the attack speed bonus does not apply and his damage is ordinary.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Trundle side lanes very well and takes towers fast, and answering him alone in his own zone rarely works.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Trundle picks are a pillar behind you at a choke, so cross corridors with a dash available or not at all.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Trundle wins by taking every duel and every objective and stealing the stats that would have answered him."},"Udyr":{"tldr":"Udyr adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Udyr seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Udyr Favored","why":"Udyr's second ability comes online too, and Wilding Claw (Q) plus Iron Mantle (W) stack attack speed, on-hit damage and self-healing that win a straight trade before your Bramble Smash (Q) is enough to offset it. Your Q knockback and slow can still punish an uncalculated invade by disengaging into a brush, but in an even skirmish Udyr's stance-swapping sustain and attack speed edge out your single peel tool."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Udyr. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Udyr's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Udyr for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Udyr's first item is where the awakened stance rotations stop being a heavy trade and start being a kill."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Udyr clears fast and healthy and wins every early fight. Blazing Stampede stuns you on his first hit every 6 seconds. Start away from his side and put a sapling at that entrance. If he runs at you, Bramble Smash knocks him back before the stun lands. Leave the crab to him. Plan a clear that ends at a level-3 gank, and counter-gank his ganks, where Twisted Advance holds him for your laner.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Udyr is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Contest scuttle against Udyr before he has two stance points, because the awakened trade pattern beats most kits at level three.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Udyr if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Udyr is a strong objective duellist with high sustained damage but no way to zone you out of the pit.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Udyr's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Udyr wins most invades fought straight, so the answer is to take his camps while he is on the far side.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Blazing Stampede only travels in a straight line, so breaking the line with terrain removes the stun entirely.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Udyr's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Udyr is weakest against distance and walls, since he has no ranged threat and no way to catch a champion who leaves.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Udyr side lanes strongly and takes towers quickly, but he cannot answer a fight happening anywhere else.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Udyr does not pick; he runs you down, so the danger is being caught in a corridor rather than being ambushed.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Udyr wins by taking every fight he can reach and every objective he can stand next to before the carries scale."},"Vi":{"tldr":"Vi adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Vi seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Vi Favored","why":"Vi's Vault Breaker (Q) dash-knockback and Denting Blows passive armor shred let her burst down a camp and threaten a level-2 all-in before your Bramble Smash (Q) is online to answer it. Your Q knockback can still punish a careless dive into a brush, but in a straight fight Vi's mobility and on-hit damage beat your single peel tool at this level."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Vi. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Vi's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Vi for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Vi's first item is where the charge-and-shred pattern stops being a chunk and starts closing out squishier junglers."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Vi wins the early fight and ganks at level 3 with Vault Breaker, which charges before it launches her. It has a 12-second cooldown. Bramble Smash knocks her back if she reaches you. Start away from her side and put a sapling in your raptor brush. Do not fight her at the crab. Counter-gank her level-3 gank: after she has used Vault Breaker, Twisted Advance roots her in place for your laner.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Vi is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Vi contests scuttle with a charged Vault Breaker, which wins the crab by moving you off it as much as by damage.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Vi if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Vi has good objective damage from the armour shred but cannot hold a pit once her charge is spent.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Vi's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Vi escapes an invade with the same charge she engages with, so the punish window is right after that Q lands.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The charge-up is visible and slow — step behind a camp while it winds and the whole engage is wasted.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Vi's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Vi is weakest with her Q on cooldown, when she is a melee champion with no gap-closer and no escape.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Vi side lanes adequately but would rather be near her team, where a point-and-click lockdown starts a fight.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Vi picks are Cease and Desist on whoever she can reach, and it cannot be dodged, so respect the health threshold.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Vi wins by locking down the one target her team needs removed and shredding the armour off it."},"Xin Zhao":{"tldr":"Xin Zhao adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Xin Zhao seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Xin Zhao Favored","why":"Xin Zhao's Three Talon Strike (Q) knocks up on the third hit and his Determination passive heals every third auto, letting him force and win a short trade before your Bramble Smash (Q) alone can out-value it. Your Q knockback still punishes a careless invade by disengaging into a brush, but a straight skirmish favors Xin Zhao's attack-reset burst at this level."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Xin Zhao. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Xin Zhao's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Xin Zhao for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Xin Zhao's first item is a sharp jump — the knock-up combo goes from a strong trade to a kill on most junglers."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Xin Zhao is much stronger than you through level 3. Audacious Charge reaches you from 650 units and slows you, and Three Talon Strike knocks you up on the third hit, so he can finish a fight before your Sap Magic heals matter. Start away from his side and put saplings at your raptors and blue. Skip the first crab, which is where he wants to fight. Counter-gank his level-3 gank with Twisted Advance, and use Bramble Smash to knock him off your laner once he has charged in.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Xin Zhao is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Contest scuttle against Xin Zhao only with a health lead — the challenge poke into the knock-up is one of the best level-three duels.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Xin Zhao if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Xin Zhao is a strong objective duellist and Crescent Guard can remove everyone but you from the pit fight.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Xin Zhao's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Xin Zhao wins invades fought straight, so the answer is to take his camps rather than to meet him at one.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The third Q hit is the knock-up — disengage after the second, and the combo has nothing left to chain.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Xin Zhao's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Xin Zhao is weakest against ranged kiting once his charge is spent, since he has nothing else that closes a gap.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Xin Zhao side lanes well early but falls behind the real side laners once full builds arrive.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Xin Zhao picks are an Audacious Charge from a bush into the knock-up, so respect river bushes on his side.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Xin Zhao wins by snowballing the early game and using Crescent Guard to make one late fight a 1v1 he chose."},"Amumu":{"tldr":"Amumu adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Amumu seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Amumu Favored","why":"At level 2 Amumu already has Bandage Toss (Q), a 1100-range stun on a 3-second cooldown, while Maokai's only tool is Bramble Smash (Q), a knockback and slow with no hard lockdown until Twisted Advance unlocks at level 3. Amumu can pull, stun, and follow up with Cursed Touch's true damage before Maokai can land a root of his own, so an early skirmish or invade favors Amumu's point-and-click CC over Maokai's disengage tool."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Amumu. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Amumu's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Amumu for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Amumu's first completed item makes Despair and Tantrum clear at real speed and turns his engage into a genuine team-fight threat."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Amumu is a tank with low early damage, and you beat him in a fight because Sap Magic heals you through his Despair. He clears well. Full clear, then take the crab near his side. Bandage Toss stuns you from 1100 units; if he lands it, answer with Twisted Advance and Bramble Smash. Put a sapling in his raptor brush, invade his second buff with your mid, and gank at level 3.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Amumu is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Amumu contests scuttle with the Bandage Toss, not with damage — if the bandage misses he loses the crab and the trade.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Amumu if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Amumu is a fast grub and drake taker because Despair burns percent health, but he needs a body between him and your smite.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Amumu's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Amumu has no dash and no escape, so an invade that starts outside bandage range is close to free.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Watch the Bandage Toss cooldown — with it down Amumu is a slow melee champion with no way to start anything.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Amumu's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Amumu is weakest between one and five, when his own duel is nothing and his ganks still need a laner to hold the target.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Amumu cannot hold a side lane; leaving him alone on a wave concedes almost nothing.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Amumu picks come from fog bandages at river entrances, so hug the wall he cannot line up against.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Amumu wins by landing one Curse of the Sad Mummy on two or more people and letting his team clean up."},"Hecarim":{"tldr":"Hecarim adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Hecarim seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Hecarim Favored","why":"Hecarim's Rampage (Q) has a 4-second base cooldown and gets cheaper and harder-hitting with every stack, so in a straight level 2 trade he out-damages Maokai's Bramble Smash (Q) before its slow can create distance. If Hecarim commits to your jungle, use Q for the knockback and disengage into a brush rather than trying to stand and trade — this window favors Hecarim on raw stacking cleave damage."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Hecarim. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Hecarim's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Hecarim for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a persistent area burn plus health that speeds up your clear and adds free damage against a melee fighter like Hecarim who has to stand in your zone to cleave with Rampage. Your camp clears become near-automatic, letting you contest neutral objectives more aggressively and hold ground in extended skirmishes where Hecarim previously out-traded you. Hecarim's first item is where the Rampage spam stops being clear speed and starts being a real health bar of damage."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Hecarim clears much faster than you and wins every early fight, and he reaches a lane at level 3 before you do. Rampage hits every 4 seconds and Spirit of Dread heals him. Devastating Charge is where your kit shines: Bramble Smash knocks him back and slows him, and Twisted Advance roots him mid-charge. Plan a clear that ends near the lane he will gank. Put a sapling in his raptor brush, and do not fight him for the crab. Counter-gank instead.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Hecarim is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Hecarim wins scuttle fights by arriving with speed already built, so the crab is usually decided before you see him.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Hecarim if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Hecarim takes objectives quickly off Rampage stacks and can disengage a lost smite fight faster than most.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Hecarim's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Hecarim is hard to catch in an invade — the passive speed and Devastating Charge mean he leaves whenever he chooses.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Watch how fast he is moving: a Hecarim already at speed is committed to a charge, and the knock-back is coming.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Hecarim's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Hecarim is weakest in a confined space with no runway, where Devastating Charge cannot build to anything.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Hecarim side lanes to build a lead and rotates faster than anyone answering him, so the wave is never the whole story.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Hecarim picks are a long charge out of fog into a knock-back against a wall, so keep open ground behind you.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Hecarim wins by reaching your back line before the fight has shape, usually through an Onslaught of Shadows fear."},"Jarvan IV":{"tldr":"Jarvan IV adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Jarvan IV seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Jarvan IV Favored","why":"Martial Cadence (P) hits Maokai hard for missing-health true-ish burst on the first auto, and Dragon Strike (Q) both knocks up and lowers armor, so Jarvan IV wins a straight trade before Bramble Smash's slow can create distance. If he commits to your jungle, use Q to knock him off you and disengage into a brush rather than standing to fight — this window favors Jarvan IV on burst, not Maokai."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Jarvan IV. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Jarvan IV's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Jarvan IV for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a persistent area burn plus health that speeds up your clear and adds free damage against a melee fighter like Jarvan IV, who has to stand in your zone to land Dragon Strike and autos. Your camp clears become near-automatic, letting you contest neutral objectives more aggressively and hold your ground in extended fights where his early burst previously won the exchange. Jarvan IV's first item makes the flag-drag rotation a genuine kill threat rather than a set-up for someone else."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Jarvan IV is favoured over you until level 6. Demacian Standard plus Dragon Strike knocks you up from about 770 units, and Golden Aegis shields him from your Bramble Smash damage. Clear from the side opposite his start, with a sapling in the raptor brush to spot his flag. He will fight you at the crab, so skip it. Counter-gank his level-3 gank: after he has used the flag, Twisted Advance roots him, and he has no way to escape.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Jarvan IV is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Jarvan IV contests scuttle with the E-Q knock-up, which wins the crab and the trade if you are standing on it.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Jarvan IV if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Jarvan IV takes objectives at a fair pace and can wall off the pit with Cataclysm so the smite fight never happens.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Jarvan IV's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Jarvan IV can flag out of most invades, so the punish window is right after that flag has been spent.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The flag is the tell — once it is on the ground the knock-up is coming, so move off the line rather than back.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Jarvan IV's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Jarvan IV is weakest once the flag is on cooldown, when he has no engage, no escape and modest damage.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Jarvan IV holds a side lane adequately but would rather be near an objective his cage can decide.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Jarvan IV picks are a flag-drag onto someone crossing a choke, so cross with a dash available or not at all.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Jarvan IV wins by caging the person who matters at the start of a fight and letting his team collapse on it."},"Maokai":{"tldr":"This is the Maokai mirror: identical Q/W/E/R tools on both sides, so nothing in the kit hands either jungler an edge. Sapling Toss (E) brush stacking, Twisted Advance (W) point-and-click lockdown, and Nature's Grasp (R) walls cancel out — the match is decided by who reads pathing and invades first, not by ability advantage. Whoever forces the other to burn W or Q defensively while behind on camps wins the tempo race.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Even","why":"Both junglers open with the same Sapling Toss (E) brush-stack clear and the same Sap Magic (P) attack-heal sustain, so the level 1 clear is symmetric — neither side gets a faster or healthier path from the kit alone. The only edge available is a leash or a scuttle-side start that gets summoner spells or first back timing slightly ahead."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Even","why":"Both sides unlock Bramble Smash (Q) at the same level with the same knockback and slow, so an invade is a coin flip decided by who arrives with more saplings up and better positioning, not by the kit — either Maokai can drag the fight into a brush and punish the other's overextension with Q."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Even","why":"Both junglers gain the same Twisted Advance (W) untargetable dash and point-and-click root at level 3, so gank strength and collapse potential are identical on both sides. Whoever lands W first in a 1v1 or a counter-gank wins the exchange — the tool itself favors neither Maokai."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Maokai's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Maokai for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Your clear runs itself, so you can sweep both sides before forcing highly aggressive neutral pit takes. This priority shatters their capacity to dive your backline evenly in river skirmishes."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Start your clear at the Raptors or Wolves camp utilizing your stacked E saplings to maximize early AoE clear speed without a leash. Monitor Maokai's opening lane arrivals to confirm their starting location, allowing you to path defensively away from their early invade vectors toward a safe full clear.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Maokai is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Maokai if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Maokai's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Maokai's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react."},"Nunu & Willump":{"tldr":"Nunu & Willump adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Nunu & Willump seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Nunu & Willump Favored","why":"Nunu & Willump's Consume (Q) heals Willump and deals true damage that ignores Maokai's resistances, and Snowball Barrage (E) is already online with a root on top of a slow — both fights he can start, not just answer. Maokai's Bramble Smash (Q) is a knockback with no root, so it can peel a following snowball but cannot stop the initial Consume-into-E burst. This skirmish window favors Nunu & Willump."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Nunu & Willump. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Nunu & Willump's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Nunu & Willump for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Nunu & Willump's first item mostly buys durability and cooldowns, so his spike is objective control rather than damage."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Nunu & Willump steals camps with Consume, but you win the fight because Sap Magic keeps you healthy and your crowd control lasts longer. Consume has a 13-second cooldown. Keep a sapling next to your buffs so you see him come to steal them. When he rolls in with the snowball, Bramble Smash knocks him back. Contest the crab, take his raptors after he ganks, and gank at level 3.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Nunu & Willump is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Nunu & Willump does not win a scuttle duel, but Consume cheats the crab out from under you in a race.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Nunu & Willump if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Nunu & Willump is the best objective thief in the game — never let a pit reach smite range with him nearby.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Nunu & Willump's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Nunu & Willump loses almost any invade fought honestly, since the kit is gank pressure rather than duel pressure.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The snowball is the tell — a rolling Nunu is committed, and stepping out of the line takes the knock-up away.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Nunu & Willump's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Nunu & Willump is weakest in a straight 1v1 at every stage of the game, which is why he never wants one.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Nunu & Willump does not side lane; he shadows objectives and waits for a smite fight he can cheat.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Nunu & Willump picks come from a long snowball out of fog, so cross river where the roll cannot build.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Nunu & Willump wins by stealing objectives and channelling Absolute Zero into a fight nobody can leave."},"Rammus":{"tldr":"Rammus adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Rammus seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Rammus Favored","why":"Rammus's Powerball (Q) closes distance fast and slows on impact, and Spiked Shell scales his attack damage off his own resistances, so he hits harder than his tank build suggests in an early trade. Maokai's Bramble Smash (Q) can knock him away, but with no root or taunt-equivalent of his own yet (Twisted Advance is still locked), he cannot force Rammus off a target the way Rammus can force a fight onto him. This skirmish window favors Rammus."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Rammus. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Rammus's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Rammus for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Rammus' first item is an armour spike more than a damage one, which is exactly what makes him harder to fight."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"You beat Rammus at levels 1 and 2, and level 3 is even. He clears slowly and ganks fast with Powerball, which has a 12-second cooldown. Bramble Smash stops a rolling Rammus, and Twisted Advance roots him from 525 units. Do not auto attack him while Defensive Ball Curl is up, since it returns damage. Take the crab on your side and put a sapling on his gank path to bot.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Rammus is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Rammus contests scuttle by rolling in and taunting — the crab is decided by whether you can leave, not by damage.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Rammus if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Rammus takes objectives slowly but defends a pit well, since a taunt at smite range ends the contest.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Rammus's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Rammus is a bad invade target for any auto-attack champion, because the reflect turns your damage back on you.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. While the ball is curled, stop attacking — every auto you throw into it is damage you take instead.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Rammus's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Rammus is weakest against ability damage and against anything that does not need to auto-attack him at all.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Rammus does not side lane; he rolls between objectives and lanes where the taunt has follow-up.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Rammus picks are a Powerball out of fog into a taunt, so watch for the roll starting long before it reaches you.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Rammus wins by locking down the person your team most needs held and surviving everything sent at him."},"Rek'Sai":{"tldr":"Rek'Sai adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Rek'Sai seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Rek'Sai Favored","why":"Rek'Sai can Burrow (W) to close distance fast and Un-burrow for a knockup, then finish with Furious Bite (E) for true damage at max Fury — a full engage-to-burst chain Maokai has no answer for until Twisted Advance comes online. Maokai's Bramble Smash (Q) can knock her back mid-chain, but the short 600 range and lack of a root means it only delays the fight rather than winning it. This skirmish window favors Rek'Sai."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Rek'Sai. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Rek'Sai's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Rek'Sai for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Rek'Sai's first item is a real jump in duel power, but it is also close to the end of her scaling curve."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Rek'Sai wins every early fight and tunnels into your jungle through paths that normal wards miss. Burrow has a 4-second cooldown, and Un-burrow knocks you up when she comes out beneath you. A sapling reveals her once she surfaces near it, so throw them beside your raptors and near the tunnel exits she leaves. Start away from her side and skip the first crab. When she ganks at level 3, Twisted Advance roots her as she comes out of the ground, and your laner does the rest.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Rek'Sai is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Rek'Sai wins scuttle contests off an Unburrow knock-up, which lands from a direction the crab fight does not expect.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Rek'Sai if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Rek'Sai is a strong objective duellist because Furious Bite is true damage and Void Rush executes the contest.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Rek'Sai's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Rek'Sai invades through her own tunnel network, so the entrances she has dug are the map you actually need warded.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Burrowed Rek'Sai shows tremors, not a champion — if she is burrowed near you the knock-up is already in range.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Rek'Sai's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Rek'Sai is weakest late, when the early tunnel pressure stops mattering and her damage falls behind the carries.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Rek'Sai side lanes to keep tunnel pressure rather than to push, so the wave is rarely why she is there.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Rek'Sai picks are an Unburrow from an unwarded tunnel, so sweep the exits rather than the bushes.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Rek'Sai wins by converting an early tunnel-tempo lead into objectives before her scaling runs out."},"Sejuani":{"tldr":"Sejuani adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Sejuani seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Bramble Smash (Q) gives you a knockback, slow, and grounding effect that shuts down Sejuani's Arctic Assault charge if she tries to engage before her own Winter's Wrath and Permafrost combo is online. If she attempts an early entry into your jungle, drag the camp toward a brush and Q her off before her Frost stacks can build toward the Permafrost stun. Neither side has hard CC ready to force a kill this early, so the disengage tool tilts the skirmish your way."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Sejuani. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Sejuani's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Sejuani for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Sejuani's first item is a durability spike — she does not start killing you, she starts being impossible to remove."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Sejuani is a slow-clearing tank like you, and you beat her early because Sap Magic keeps you healthier through your route. Arctic Assault knocks you up from 650 units. Winter's Wrath slows you from 600 units and builds Frost for her stun, so answer with Bramble Smash, which slows her back. Full clear, take the crab, and put a sapling on her gank route. When she heads to a lane, invade her second camp with your mid, and gank the opposite lane at level 3.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Sejuani is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Sejuani contests scuttle with Permafrost stuns rather than damage — she wins the crab when a laner is with her.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Sejuani if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Sejuani takes objectives slowly but holds a pit very well, since Glacial Prison starts the fight on her terms.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Sejuani's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Sejuani survives an invade far better than she wins one; expect her to walk out rather than die in it.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Her passive frost armour is the tell — a Sejuani who has not taken damage recently is much harder to burst.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Sejuani's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Sejuani is weakest as a solo damage threat, since almost everything she does needs a teammate to finish.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Sejuani does not side lane; she plays the river and the pits, where a cross-map stun is worth more than a wave.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Sejuani picks are a long Glacial Prison from fog, so keep terrain between you and the direction she disappeared.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Sejuani wins by landing one cross-map stun into a fight her team is already positioned for."},"Skarner":{"tldr":"Skarner adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Skarner seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"At level 2 Skarner only has Shattered Earth (Q), a short 400-range poke with no hard CC — his stun on Ixtal's Impact (E) and suppress on Impale (R) are both still locked. Bramble Smash (Q) grounds him out of any dash attempt and knocks him off before he can stack Quaking, so a drag-to-brush disengage wins the trade cleanly. Neither champion can force a kill yet, but Maokai's peel tool is live first."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Skarner. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Skarner's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Skarner for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Skarner's first item buys the health and cooldowns that let him survive the answer to his own engage."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Skarner wins the early fight and ganks with Ixtal's Impact, which carries a target along until it hits a wall. Seismic Bastion shields him on a 10-second cooldown. Bramble Smash can knock him away before he grabs your laner, and Twisted Advance roots him mid-charge. Start away from his side, put a sapling at your raptors, and skip the first crab. Counter-gank his ganks, and stay away from walls when he is near, because the jungle gives him plenty to slam you into.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Skarner is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Skarner contests scuttle with the wall stun — near terrain the crab fight is his, and in the open it is not.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Skarner if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Skarner holds a pit well because Impale removes whoever steps up to contest the smite.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Skarner's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Skarner is durable enough to survive an invade but slow enough that he cannot punish one that leaves.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Terrain is the tell — Skarner near a wall has a stun, and Skarner in the open river has a slow and nothing else.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Skarner's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Skarner is weakest in open ground, where Ixtal's Impact cannot connect anyone to a wall.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Skarner does not side lane; he plays the pits, where a suppression on the right target decides the objective.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Skarner picks are an Impale on whoever walks a corridor first, so never lead a rotation through a choke.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Skarner wins by dragging one key target into his team at the start of every objective fight."},"Volibear":{"tldr":"Volibear adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Volibear seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Volibear Favored","why":"Volibear's Thundering Smash (Q) closes distance and stuns off his own auto-attack, and his passive Attack Speed stacking lets him burst a level-2 fight before your Bramble Smash (Q) alone can offset it. Your Q knockback still punishes a careless dive by disengaging into a brush, but in an even trade Volibear's gap-closing stun wins the skirmish at this level."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Volibear. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Volibear's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Volibear for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Volibear's first item is where the bite trades stop being even and start ending with your health bar gone."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Volibear beats you in any early fight. Thundering Smash speeds him toward you and stuns on his first attack, and Frenzied Maul heals him every 5 seconds. Start away from his side and put a sapling at your blue. If he runs at you, Bramble Smash knocks him back before he reaches you. Skip the crab, where his stun wins. Counter-gank his level-3 gank: once he has dived, Twisted Advance roots him under your tower. Base after that fight so you are healthy before he comes back.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Volibear is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Volibear takes most scuttle contests — the stun into the maul bite pattern is one of the better level-three duels.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Volibear if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Volibear is a strong objective duellist and can disable a tower afterwards, so a won pit fight costs you twice.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Volibear's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Volibear wins most invades fought straight, and the Q stun means walking away from him is not simple.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Thundering Smash has to run at you in a straight line, so a wall or a camp between you removes the stun.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Volibear's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Volibear is weakest against ranged kiting, since he has no way to close a gap before six.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Volibear side lanes strongly and takes towers fast, which makes ignoring the wave expensive.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Volibear picks are a Stormbringer leap onto a group, so spread off the landing marker rather than running from it.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Volibear wins by brawling through the mid game and using the ultimate to take towers and objectives on the same trip."},"Warwick":{"tldr":"Warwick adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Warwick seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Warwick Favored","why":"Warwick's Jaws of the Beast (Q) is a percent-max-health lunge that heals him for the damage dealt, and Eternal Hunger stacks extra sustain once either of you drops low — a healthy trade he wins before your Bramble Smash (Q) alone can out-damage his lifesteal. Your Q knockback can still punish a careless dive by disengaging into a brush, but a straight fight favors Warwick's sustain at this level."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Warwick. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Warwick's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Warwick for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Warwick's first item is where the healing stops being sustain and starts being more than your damage can out-pace."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Warwick wins every early fight and heals more when he is low. Blood Hunt senses anyone below half health, so keep your health up with Sap Magic during your clear. Jaws of the Beast bites from 365 units every 8 seconds. Start on the buff away from him and keep a sapling at raptors so you see him come. Skip the crab. When he counter-ganks your laners, Twisted Advance holds him in place and Bramble Smash knocks him away from your low laner.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Warwick is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Contest scuttle against Warwick from full health, since his sustain out-lasts what most junglers can put out at that timer.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Warwick if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Warwick is a strong objective duellist with the Q percent-health damage and can hold the pit through a long fight.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Warwick's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Invade Warwick above fifty percent health, since below it his sustain and Blood Hunt win the fight your invade was meant to end.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Blood Hunt tracks anyone below half; if your health drops he gets speed toward you and you will not out-run it.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Warwick's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Warwick is weakest above fifty percent health, where the tracking does not apply and his damage is much lower.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Warwick side lanes adequately but his real value is answering any low-health fight anywhere on the map.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Warwick picks are an Infinite Duress onto whoever is lowest, so recall earlier than feels necessary against him.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Warwick wins by suppressing a carry for the length of a fight while out-healing everything sent back at him."},"Zac":{"tldr":"Zac adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Zac seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Zac Favored","why":"Zac's Stretching Strikes (Q) pulls and slings you in for burst on top of Unstable Matter's (W) percent-health nuke, and Cell Division lets him shed and reabsorb blobs to sustain through the trade — pressure your Bramble Smash (Q) alone can't out-value yet. Your Q knockback can still punish a careless dive by disengaging into a brush, but a straight fight favors Zac's percent-health burst and passive sustain at this level."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Zac. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Zac's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Zac for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Zac's first item is a durability spike, so he does not start killing you — he starts surviving whatever you send."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Zac clears slowly like you, but you win the early fight because Sap Magic heals you and his damage is low. Elastic Slingshot has a 21-second cooldown and is his only engage, so once he has launched, he cannot chase. Bramble Smash knocks him back as he lands on your laner. Full clear, take both crabs, and put a sapling near his raptors. Step on the blobs he drops so he cannot heal from them, and gank at level 3.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Zac is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Zac contests scuttle with a Slingshot from fog rather than a duel — the crab goes to whoever gets knocked up.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Zac if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Zac takes objectives slowly but survives a pit fight better than most, and the passive blob buys a second attempt.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Zac's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Zac loses a straight invade but is hard to kill in one, so an invade against him usually costs more time than it earns.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The Slingshot charge is visible from far away — ward the walls he charges from and the engage never lands.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Zac's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Zac is weakest when his E is on cooldown and his passive blob has already been used, which is a long window.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Zac does not side lane; he lurks near objectives where a fog engage is worth more than a wave.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Zac picks are a long-range Slingshot into a group, so hold a dash for the landing rather than for the follow-up.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Zac wins by starting objective fights from fog with his whole team behind him and two health bars in front."},"Brand":{"tldr":"Brand adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Brand seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Brand Favored","why":"Brand's Sear (Q) stuns on hit against an ablaze target and Pillar of Flame (W) hits from 900 range, so he can stack Blaze and land a stun before Maokai closes the gap — Twisted Advance's root is still a level away. Bramble Smash only has 600 range and no way to interrupt Brand's poke from a distance, so an early skirmish or invade at level 2 favors Brand's ranged burst over Maokai's short-range tools."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Brand. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Brand's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Brand for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Brand's first item makes the Sear-into-Conflagration rotation a full health bar on a squishy, with no auto-attacks needed."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Brand wins the early fight with more burst than a level-3 tank can take, but he has no escape. He clears fast with Pillar of Flame and often ganks at level 3. Sear stuns you from 1050 units if you are already burning. Put a sapling on his gank path and counter-gank: Twisted Advance is untargetable while you dash, so you reach him through his spells. Skip the first crab if he is near. Recall once the counter-gank is over, before his mana is back.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Brand is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Brand wins a scuttle contest at range rather than at the crab — he will chip you off it instead of fighting for it.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Brand if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Brand melts objectives with the Blaze percent-health burn, so treat any pit he has vision on as contested.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Brand's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Brand has no dash at all, so an invade that arrives from two directions ends with him dead in his own jungle.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Watch his mana and the Sear cooldown — a Brand without the stun cannot start anything on you.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Brand's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Brand is weakest when Sear is on cooldown and there is only one target near him for Conflagration to bounce from.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Brand does not hold a side lane; he shoves it and leaves, so answer the wave and not the champion.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Brand picks are a Sear from fog into the full rotation, so break line of sight at the entrances he likes.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Brand wins by dragging fights next to his laners where Pyroclasm can bounce, and by burning objectives faster than you can."},"Fiddlesticks":{"tldr":"Fiddlesticks adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Fiddlesticks seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Unlocking Bramble Smash (Q) gives you a knockback and slow that speeds up your clear and covers your camps against an early invade. Fiddlesticks is still squishy and has no reliable escape at this level beyond Terrify's fear, so if it commits to your jungle, drag the camp to a brush and use Q to knock it off you while your saplings whittle its health. This window favors Maokai — Fiddlesticks only becomes a real skirmish threat once it can find an unseen Terrify cast or has levels for Reap's silence."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Fiddlesticks. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Fiddlesticks's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Fiddlesticks for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a persistent area burn plus health that dramatically speeds up your clear and punishes Fiddlesticks for standing near you in a skirmish. Your camp clears become near-automatic, letting you contest neutral objectives more aggressively than Fiddlesticks, whose damage still depends on landing Reap or committing to a Crowstorm channel. This priority makes it harder for Fiddlesticks to dive past you onto your backline in river fights. Fiddlesticks' first item turns the drain and the fear into a real health bar of damage rather than a delay."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Fiddlesticks clears slowly, and you win the early fight because you are too tanky for his drain. He places effigies that look like wards. Terrify fears you from 575 units, but Twisted Advance roots him from 525, so walk in with Bramble Smash first. Sapling Toss in brush reveals him if he waits there. Full clear, take the crab, invade his raptors with your mid, and gank at level 3.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Fiddlesticks is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Fiddlesticks avoids a straight scuttle contest before six and would rather bluff you off the crab with an effigy.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Fiddlesticks if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Fiddlesticks drains objectives quickly, but the channel means any interruption costs him the whole attempt.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Fiddlesticks's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Fiddlesticks is very weak to an early invade — he has no mobility and the drain is cancelled by any damage.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. The effigies are free information: a fake Fiddlesticks in your jungle usually means the real one is somewhere else.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Fiddlesticks's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Fiddlesticks is weakest before six, when Terrify is his only real answer and his clear has left him low.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Fiddlesticks does not side lane; he sits in fog near an objective and waits for the fight to come to him.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Fiddlesticks picks are a Crowstorm out of unwarded fog, so deep wards are worth more than any item against him.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Fiddlesticks wins by landing one unseen ultimate into a grouped fight, which is why vision is the entire counter-play."},"Gragas":{"tldr":"Gragas adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Gragas seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Unlocking Bramble Smash (Q) gives you a knockback and slow that speeds up your clear and covers your camps against an early invade. Gragas at this level only has Barrel Roll (Q) or Drunken Rage (W) online, neither of which threatens burst damage, so if he commits to your jungle, drag the camp to a brush and use Q to shove him off while saplings whittle his health. This window favors Maokai — Gragas only becomes a real skirmish threat once Body Slam (E) is available for its stun."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Gragas. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Gragas's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Gragas for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a persistent area burn plus health that speeds up your clear and punishes Gragas for standing near you once Drunken Rage is active. Your camp clears become near-automatic, letting you contest neutral objectives more aggressively than Gragas, whose damage still depends on landing Barrel Roll or Body Slam. This priority makes it harder for Gragas to dive past you onto your backline in river fights. Gragas' first item makes the Barrel Roll into Body Slam combo a real chunk instead of a nuisance, on a short cooldown."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Gragas has the stronger early fight. Body Slam stuns you and has a 14-second cooldown, and Barrel Roll pokes you from 850 units before you can reach him. Start where he is least likely to roll a barrel in, and leave a sapling at your raptors. His barrel pokes you off the crab, so skip it. He usually ganks at level 3, so plan your clear to finish near his likely lane and counter-gank: after Body Slam is used, Twisted Advance roots him. Bramble Smash knocks him away from your laner. Recall after the fight.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Gragas is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Gragas contests scuttle with the Body Slam knock-back — he does not need to win the fight, only to move you off it.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Gragas if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Gragas is an awkward objective fighter alone, but with Explosive Cask he can simply remove you from the pit.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Gragas's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Gragas escapes an invade with Body Slam through a wall, so the punish window is the seconds after that E lands.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Watch the Body Slam cooldown — it is his engage and his escape, and he cannot have both at once.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Gragas's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Gragas is weakest with his E down, since his damage without the slam engage is poke and nothing else.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Gragas shoves a side lane with Barrel Roll and rotates rather than duelling, so he is often two waves from the fight.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Gragas picks are a Cask from over a wall that separates one person from the group at a choke.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Gragas wins by displacing whoever matters most at the moment the fight starts, usually around an objective pit."},"Ivern":{"tldr":"Ivern adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Ivern seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Ivern has no real damage at this point — Rootcaller (Q) is a root/dash-enabler for allies, not a threat on its own, and Triggerseed (E) is a shield/slow for a carry rather than a duel tool, since Ivern's passive prevents him from attacking or being attacked by camps in the first place. Unlocking Bramble Smash (Q) lets you knock him off you and shred saplings into his health bar if he wanders in without backup. This window favors Maokai — Ivern alone simply cannot win a fight, only enable one."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Ivern. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Ivern's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Ivern for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a persistent area burn plus health that speeds up your clear and adds free damage in any skirmish. Ivern still contributes no personal damage to a fight, so this priority mainly lets you out-farm his freed camps and contest neutral objectives before his empowered allies can punish you. Ivern's first item mostly buys shield strength and cooldowns — his spike is what his carry does, not what he does."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Ivern cannot fight monsters and is the weakest duelist in the game, so you win easily. He marks camps with Friend of the Forest and collects them later, which lets you take his marked camps. Walk into his jungle at level 3. Rootcaller reaches 1125 units, much farther than your spells, so walk around its line. Twisted Advance and Bramble Smash kill him. Take both crabs, put saplings at his buffs, and gank a lane after the steal.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Ivern is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Ivern does not contest scuttle on his own; if he shows at the crab, a laner is already in the river with him.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Ivern if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Ivern takes objectives with a free smite and Daisy as the body, so treat a pit he has vision on as a two-body fight.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Ivern's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Ivern loses any invade he is caught alone in — the kit is a root and a shield, with nothing that trades back.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Watch the marked camps: a freed camp on your side of the map means Ivern has already been there and left.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Ivern's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Ivern is weakest in any straight 1v1, at every level, which is why he never takes one voluntarily.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Ivern does not hold a side lane; he shields whoever does and makes that lane a 2v1 you have to answer with numbers.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Ivern picks are a Rootcaller onto a target his team can reach, so stay outside root range of his brush.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Ivern wins by making his carry unkillable and his jungle free, which is a tempo lead rather than a fighting one."},"Karthus":{"tldr":"Karthus adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Karthus seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Karthus has no mobility and no hard CC at this level — Lay Waste (Q) is a delayed skillshot and Wall of Pain (W) only slows, so he cannot stop Twisted Advance (W) or Bramble Smash's knockback from closing the gap or peeling him off. Unlocking Bramble Smash (Q) lets you knock him back and shred saplings into his health bar if he is caught without support. This window favors Maokai — Karthus wins from range, not in a straight melee fight."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Karthus. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Karthus's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Karthus for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a persistent area burn plus health that speeds up your clear and adds free damage if Karthus is caught in melee range, though the bigger risk from Karthus is Requiem (R) hitting your whole team from anywhere on the map. This priority mainly helps you contest neutral objectives faster and hold ground once you close the distance on him. Karthus' first item is where Lay Waste stops chipping and starts taking a third of a health bar per cast."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Karthus farms faster than anyone but cannot fight early, so you win. His full clear puts him on his second buff or raptors just after level 3. Invade there with your mid and open with Twisted Advance from 525 units. Lay Waste has 875 range and almost no cooldown, so do not stand still. Wall of Pain slows you, so walk around it. Take both crabs, put a sapling at his buffs, and recall after the kill so he stays behind.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Karthus is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Karthus rarely contests a scuttle directly — he takes the free one and out-farms you for the one he skipped.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Karthus if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Karthus melts objectives with Defile, so a pit he reaches first is usually gone before you arrive.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Karthus's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Karthus is very weak to an early invade, but Death Defied means killing him still costs you the following seconds.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Watch Wall of Pain: once it is placed he is committed to the fight, and before that he is only farming.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Karthus's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Karthus is weakest at melee range, where the isolated Lay Waste bonus does not apply and he cannot walk away.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Karthus shoves side waves faster than anyone but has no answer to a champion who arrives while he is doing it.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Karthus does not pick; he finishes, so the danger is the recall you take at low health rather than the fight.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Karthus wins by out-farming the map and cashing that lead into Requiem finishes on fights he was never in."},"Lillia":{"tldr":"Lillia adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Lillia seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Bramble Smash (Q) comes online with a 7-second cooldown, giving you a real knockup and slow to punish a contested camp. At level 2 Lillia only has Blooming Blows (Q), a short-range true-damage nuke with no CC and no dash, so she has no way to close distance safely or escape once you land Q. If she tries an uncalculated invade, drag the camp to a brush and let Q knock her off while saplings chip her health."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Lillia. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Lillia's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Lillia for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Lillia's first item is when the passing damage becomes real — she stops chipping and starts out-pacing health bars."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Lillia is even with you at levels 1 and 2, and at level 3 her speed makes her the favourite. She clears fast and kites. Swirlseed slows you from 700 units, and Blooming Blows gives her speed on a 6-second cooldown. Twisted Advance is your only way to reach her. Full clear, share the crabs before level 3, and put a sapling at her raptors. From level 3, gank lanes instead of chasing her.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Lillia is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Lillia contests scuttle by out-running you to it rather than by fighting, and leaves the moment you commit.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Lillia if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Lillia has good objective damage but a poor pit fight, so contest her smite attempts with a body rather than a race.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Lillia's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Lillia is hard to invade because the passive movement speed means she is gone before the second person arrives.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Swirlseed is her commitment: once the seed lands she will fight, and before that she is only kiting.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Lillia's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Lillia is weakest against anything that can close the gap on her, since her damage is over time and not burst.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Lillia side lanes fast and disengages faster, so answering her alone with a slow champion accomplishes nothing.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Lillia picks come from a rolled Swirlseed down a corridor, so cross river in the open rather than through a lane.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Lillia wins by kiting until her dream-dust damage adds up and using the sleep to make one fight unfair."},"Morgana":{"tldr":"Morgana adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Morgana seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Morgana Favored","why":"Morgana's Dark Binding (Q) is a 1250-range hard root online at level 2 — far outranging your 600-range Bramble Smash — so she can lock you down from well outside your own engage range before you ever get to swing Q. Getting rooted into a contested camp or an invade leaves you eating free damage with no answer until the root breaks. Play passively around her Q range here rather than trying to force the trade."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Morgana. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Morgana's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Morgana for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Morgana's first item makes the binding-into-zone rotation a real chunk instead of a set-up for somebody else."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Morgana clears very fast with Tormented Shadow but has no escape, so you win the early fight. Dark Binding roots from 1250 units, twice the range of anything you have. Black Shield blocks your root if she casts it first, so bait it with Bramble Smash. Invade her second camp at level 3 with your mid, and open with Twisted Advance once her shield is used. Take both crabs, put saplings at her buffs, and recall after the kill.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Morgana is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Morgana contests scuttle with a Dark Binding rather than a duel — the crab is lost to the root, not to the damage.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Morgana if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Morgana takes objectives quickly with the W but has almost no answer to a contested smite fight.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Morgana's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Morgana is very weak to an early invade, since Black Shield stops the crowd control and not the damage.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Dark Binding is the whole threat; sidestep it and she has a slow zone and a shield, neither of which kills you.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Morgana's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Morgana is weakest away from her laners, where the binding has no follow-up and the ultimate has no second target.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Morgana does not side lane; she plays beside a carry, where the shield turns a 2v2 into a 2v1.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Morgana picks are a binding from fog into her team, so cross the river outside her range or with a body between.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Morgana wins by making one fight near her team unwinnable — a shield on the right person and a root on the wrong one."},"Taliyah":{"tldr":"Taliyah adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Taliyah seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Taliyah Favored","why":"Taliyah's Threaded Volley (Q) hits from 1000 range while she moves freely mid-cast, and you have no gap-closer until Twisted Advance (W) unlocks at level 3 — Bramble Smash (Q) only reaches 600. She can poke down your clear or a duel from outside your kit's range entirely, and her Q also grounds any dash you might have. Play this window passively and wait for W before contesting her directly."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Taliyah. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Taliyah's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Taliyah for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Taliyah's first item makes the volley-into-shove combo a real burst instead of chip damage from range."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Taliyah's burst beats you early. She clears very fast with Threaded Volley and ganks at level 3 with Seismic Shove into Unraveled Earth. Twisted Advance is a dash, so using it through her mines stuns you; wait for them to explode first. Put a sapling on her gank path and counter-gank the lane she visits. Her W throws you off the crab, so leave it. Her W has a 14-second cooldown, so root her after she has shoved someone. Back after the fight, and watch for her roaming the other way next.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Taliyah is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Taliyah contests scuttle at range with the volley and the shove rather than by standing on the crab.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Taliyah if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Taliyah has good objective damage but a poor pit fight, so contest her smite attempts rather than racing them.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Taliyah's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Taliyah is weak to an early invade, since Seismic Shove is her only escape and it needs to land to work.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Worked ground is visible — she does far less damage on it, so fighting on ground she has already used is safer.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Taliyah's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Taliyah is weakest at close range with the shove spent, where she has no way to make distance again.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Taliyah shoves side waves and crosses the map on her passive, so she is rarely where the wave says she is.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Taliyah picks are a shove into her team or a wall behind you, so watch which direction she is facing before you cross.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Taliyah wins by splitting the map with Weaver's Wall and zoning the fight her team chooses to take."},"Zyra":{"tldr":"Zyra adjusts your early mechanical tempo by requiring high precision over Sapling Toss (E) bush placement and Twisted Advance (W) lockdown timing. Maokai completely commands skirmish velocities through unmatched area control, using Bramble Smash (Q) to peel, and deploying Nature's Grasp (R) to execute massive teamfight roots. Zyra seeks to out-duel your early clearing tempo, aggressively counter-jungle your camps, and exploit your dependency on your team's damage output.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Maokai Favored","why":"Start with Sapling Toss (E) stacked inside a brush to secure a fast area-of-effect clear, utilizing the empowered explosion to melt the camp. Execute basic attacks to trigger your Sap Magic passive for immediate health sustain. This establishes an exceptionally fast and healthy path across your starting camps."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Zyra Favored","why":"Zyra's Grasping Roots (E) roots from 1100 range and Deadly Spines (Q) adds zoning damage on top, letting her poke and root you from well outside your Bramble Smash (Q) engage range. Your Q knockback only matters once she is already in melee range, so at range Zyra's root-and-poke kit wins the skirmish at this level."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Maokai Scaling Peak","why":"Access to Twisted Advance (W) completes your lethal ganking portfolio by introducing a versatile untargetable dash and point-and-click root. Use this tool to instantly secure your crowd control chains or lock down targets during deep jungle collapses executed by Zyra. This grants an elite operational flexibility allowing you to turn almost any early brawl to your advantage through sheer lockdown."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Tempo Generation Focus","why":"Your basic abilities now hit severe early utility thresholds, letting you dictate the entire tempo of map pressure through hyper-aggressive lane ganks and objective zoning. Keep constant tabs on Zyra's jungle clearing lines and use your Saplings to heavily scout their pathing inside dark jungle zones. Prioritize rapid champion lockdowns and snowballing your laners over executing slow, forced horizontal plays."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Nature's Grasp Domination","why":"Nature's Grasp (R) introduces an immense combat ultimate that provides a massive advancing wall of roots to completely control the battlefield. Execute a precise W engage before casting Nature's Grasp to permanently isolate and entangle Zyra for a clean kill. This massive mechanical spike turns you into an absolute map-wide teamfight threat who dictates the shape of every fight through guaranteed initiation."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Sunfire Aegis Spike","why":"Completing Sunfire Aegis gives you a constant area burn plus health, so camps melt while you stand in them and every extended fight with a brawler has free damage ticking on your side. Zyra's first item is where plant damage stops being chip and starts removing a squishy health bar in one rotation."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Unstoppable Vanguard","why":"Maokai transitions into a high-impact teamfight vanguard who relies entirely on generating immense pressure by locking down the enemy cluster and absorbing damage. Your late-game raw damage scaling shifts into a purely frontline-engage line, making it imperative to use your ultimate to zone multiple threats simultaneously. Maintain extreme technical precision around high-tier objective pits to cleanly initiate on the contesting team and secure the objective with unparalleled AoE CC."}],"start":"Zyra is the stronger side early. Her plants help her clear and hit you hard in any long trade. Grasping Roots roots you from 1100 units, and Deadly Spines grows a plant from each seed. Put saplings in brush near your lanes so you see her seeds. Her seeds cover the crab pit, so do not fight there. She has no escape, so counter-gank her ganks: Twisted Advance is untargetable while you dash, so you reach her through her plants. Bramble Smash knocks her plants back too.","scuttle":"Secure the scuttle crab the moment it spawns, using your W-Q combo to instantly drop its resistance shield and establish river control. Avoid soloing early Dragons unless Zyra is explicitly confirmed dead, as you are highly vulnerable to being collapsed upon while your abilities are on cooldown. Zyra contests scuttle on seeds — a crab fight over a grown plant garden is one she wins without duelling.","topObj":"Force the fight for early Void Grubs the exact moment they spawn, using your AoE smash and saplings to melt the camp rapidly. Use your W to cleanly lock down and stall Zyra if they dare to contest, buying time for your top laner to rotate. Zyra melts objectives with plant damage but has no way to survive a pit fight she does not control.","invade":"You do not aggressively invade Zyra's jungle in the early game unless accompanied by lane priority and a strong skirmishing mid laner. Rely on counter-ganking by using Twisted Advance from the fog of war to turn their aggressive plays against them. Zyra is very weak to an early invade away from her seeds, since the kit has no escape at all.","watch":"Watch for sudden counter-invades relentlessly during your first clear, especially at your second buff. This leaves you extremely vulnerable, so you must pull the buff into the brush and save your Q strictly for a disengage knockback if engaged upon. Seeds on the ground are the whole warning — kill them on sight and her damage drops by more than half.","weak":"When your side lanes lose priority, play defensively by abandoning contested scuttle crabs and using Saplings to safely trace Zyra's routes. Funnel entirely into hyper-farming your multi-target camps to reach your item and Ultimate power spikes so you can transition into an unstoppable lockdown vanguard. Zyra is weakest with no seeds down and her root on cooldown, which is when she has nothing at all.","split":"You cannot split push safely due to your lack of escape mobility and slow tower damage, meaning your sole role is generating massive map pressure through teamfights. In teamfights, you must act as the primary hard engage, utilizing your W-Q-R combo to completely lock down their clustered formation. Zyra does not side lane; she plays near her team where the plants and the knock-up cover a choke.","picks":"Focus entirely on hunting for clustered enemies or playing front-to-back by launching your ultimate to peel for your carries if dived. In standard group fights, look to execute a ruthless forward push, completely locking their frontline and backline simultaneously to instantly win the teamfight. Zyra picks are a Grasping Roots through a choke into a seeded garden, so never walk a corridor she has covered.","win":"Generate an unanswerable mid-to-late game teamfight dominance through successful point-and-click ganks and absolute maximum crowd control layering. Close the match by relentlessly engaging 5v5 teamfights around major objectives, forcing the enemy team to face an unstoppable advancing root wall before they can react. Zyra wins by covering an objective choke in plants and ending any fight taken inside it with the knock-up."},"Locke":{"tldr":"Locke is a weak-side execute jungler: he loses honest duels and farms for the totem — fight him marks-zero, punish every spent blink, and never coin-flip at sliver HP inside Purgatory.","stages":[{"stage":"Level 1 Clear","adv":"Respect Locke","why":"Locke clears at range but pays mana and HP for it — his level 1 duel is honest only with nail marks banked, so fight him marks-zero. take the contact trades your kit is built for."},{"stage":"Level 2 Skirmish","adv":"Respect Locke","why":"His E is engage AND escape in one button: the ten seconds after his blink are your whole trade window. take the contact trades your kit is built for."},{"stage":"Level 3 Route","adv":"Even Skirmish","why":"Full kits online — sidestep the Q recasts and his burst never adds up; nature's Grasp roots the whole river — objective fights start on your terms."},{"stage":"Levels 4-5 Macro","adv":"Even — Farm & Track","why":"Levels 4-5 vs Locke are a tempo war — he wants quiet farm into his 6; tax his camps, keep his marks off you at contests, and bank the gold lead his weak duel hands you."},{"stage":"Level 6 Breakpoint","adv":"Even Ult Window","why":"Purgatory executes anyone who fights below the floor inside his totem — disengage above ~15% or leave the zone entirely. Outside the totem, nature's Grasp roots the whole river — objective fights start on your terms."},{"stage":"First Item Spike","adv":"Lich Bane / Shadowflame Spike","why":"His Lich Bane spike is real: the blink-consume chunk one-shots chipped squishies. Respect the first-item window, then note his E is still a one-way door — punish every spent blink. Locke's first item makes the mark consume hit for a real chunk, which is when the blink stops being a poke tool."},{"stage":"2+ Items Scaling","adv":"Maokai Favored at Full Build","why":"Late-game Locke scales with his seal count, not his items — deny executes and his floor never rises. Focus him when E is down and never let \"almost dead\" linger inside Purgatory."}],"start":"Locke out-damages you before your items. Ritual Nails marks you from 950 units, and Ashen Pursuit blinks him in to cash those marks, on a 10-second cooldown. Begin your route away from his start and throw a sapling into your raptor brush, so his blink never surprises you. The crab is where he banks marks on you, so skip it. His blink is his only escape, so counter-gank him: wait for Ashen Pursuit, then Twisted Advance roots him and Bramble Smash slows him. Recall after the fight.","scuttle":"Take the first scuttle contact with confidence unless you’re carrying 2-3 nail marks — his blink-consume needs stacks, so an unmarked contest is yours. Locke contests scuttle on his mark count, not his health bar — an unmarked contest is much safer than a stacked one.","topObj":"Contest grubs and herald hard pre-6; post-6, watch the totem drop — leave the pit above the execute floor and re-enter when it expires. Locke takes objectives adequately and the Purgatory totem makes a late pit fight far more dangerous than the numbers suggest.","invade":"Invade the side he shows opposite — his escape is one blink on a ten-second timer, and a Locke who spends E on a camp is a kill waiting for a ping. Locke escapes an invade with one blink on a ten-second timer, so an invade into a spent Ashen Pursuit is close to free.","watch":"Watch your own mark stacks like a cooldown: a stacked nail string into the blink-through consume deletes half a health bar — drop stacks before they mature. Watch your own mark stacks like a cooldown — a full nail string into the blink consume takes half a health bar.","weak":"If he executes a lane early, respect the seal — his floor just rose permanently. Deny the snowball by matching his ganks cross-map rather than duelling into his counter-window. Locke is weakest in the seconds after his blink is spent, when he is a ranged champion standing in melee range.","split":"He punishes greedy splitters with totem duels — send answers in pairs or shove the opposite side; solo brawls at low HP are exactly the fight he farms. Locke side lanes well because the totem punishes anyone who answers him at anything less than full health.","picks":"His picks start with a nail string from fog — hug vision lines, and treat three stacks plus his E up as a dive threat even at even HP. Locke picks start with a nail string from fog, so hug vision lines and treat three stacks as a dive threat.","win":"Win the duel war he keeps declining: invade the spent-blink windows, end fights above his floor, and starve the seal count that his whole late game is built on. Locke wins by raising his execute floor with every seal until low-health fights inside his totem stop being survivable."}};
